{"text":"Recent work favors evaluating individual explanation claims against evidence. “On the Factual Consistency of Text-based Explainable Recommendation Models” (2025) reports high semantic similarity despite poor factual precision; its review-based evidence is unavailable here, but statement-level verification transfers to titles and descriptions. “Function-based Labels for Complementary Recommendation” (2025) distinguishes substitutes, replenishment, and functional dependencies, warning that behavioral co-occurrence produces noisy relationship labels. This supports probes that identify a concrete joint task and cite the product text establishing each item’s role. [Factual consistency](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.24366), [Function-based labels](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.03945).

Abstention research treats insufficient evidence as a reason to withhold judgment. A suitable adaptation is supported / contradicted / unknown for each compatibility or prerequisite claim, retaining the evidence span and missing requirement. Missing specifications mean unknown compatibility; an absent prerequisite in recorded history does not establish nonownership. Similarly, functional similarity can flag a possible redundant purchase, but cannot establish replacement intent, continuing ownership, or lack of need. These are proposed operational rules, rather than validated conclusions for this benchmark. Report both judgment coverage and error among resolved judgments. [Know Your Limits (2025)](https://aclanthology.org/2025.tacl-1.26/).

Evaluate factual support separately from the twenty users’ expressed usefulness and purchase intent. A 2025 controlled study found explanations increased reliance on incorrect as well as correct answers, showing why agreement alone cannot establish grounding. For this small feedback sample, retain explicit acceptance, rejection, uncertainty, and missing feedback separately; report counts, category breakdowns, and disagreements without inferring diversity or conformity traits. Treat these diagnostics as exploratory alongside macro validation HR@10. [Appropriate reliance study](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.08554)."}
